{{ macros.img_link("repl-debugger.gif", mkdocs, "520px") }}

`tux.repl.cmk` provides a reusable, multi-region terminal **REPL**[^repl] over a program's make-targets: a
scrolling output **panel**, a **text-area** prompt, a one-line **mode-line**, and optional right-hand
**minimaps**.  Type a target name and it runs; its output streams into the panel.  More broadly it is a
metadata-driven TUI host, reusable for non-debugger clients (see *Beyond a make-REPL* below).

It's the engine behind the [zero-config REPL]({{_}}/zero-config-tuis/#interactive-repl)
you get from `cmk repl` or the `repl` pragma, and it is also directly reusable to build a *custom* REPL
wired to your own read / eval / print targets.

{{ macros.terminology("A **read-eval-print loop** is an interactive prompt that **read**s an input, **eval**uates it, **print**s the result, and **loop**s.  Here the three verbs are ordinary make-targets: the prompt's input is fed to your `eval` target, whose output (and the `print` target's) streams into the panel, so the loop is wired from your own program's targets rather than a fixed interpreter.", title="**Definition: REPL**") }}

#### Three ways to get a REPL
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

**1. `cmk repl` (no wiring).**  Open a shell over any program's target namespace; or the core
namespace with no file:

```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./compose.mk cmk repl                         # a shell over the core namespace
$ ./compose.mk cmk repl demos/cmk/overlay.cmk   # a shell over a program's targets
```

**2. The `repl` pragma (default to a REPL).**  A program can make the REPL its *default* action, so a
bare `cmk run <file>` launches it.  The pragma is the **whole** wiring: no import, no `tux.repl`
boilerplate (the runtime owns the harness; the file owns only its targets):

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/repl.cmk', type='cmk', github=github)}}

**3. The plugin building blocks (custom REPL).**  Import the plugin and `tux.repl.declare` to bind
three of *your own* targets to the three regions:

```cmk {.language-cmk}
$(call __plugins__.require, tux.repl.cmk)
$(call tux.repl.declare, namespace=ui read=my.read eval=my.eval print=my.print)
```

...which wires a `ui` target whose recipe launches the REPL.  (The underlying callable is the
`tux.repl` macro, usable directly as `cmk.tux.repl(..)` on a recipe line if you prefer.)

#### Three regions
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

| Region | Target | Behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **panel** (top, scrolling) | `print` | called with no args; its stdout/stderr stream onto the panel |
| **prompt** (text-area) | `eval` | a stdin sink: each submission is written to its stdin; eval's own stdout/stderr also stream to the panel: the read-eval-**print** loop |
| **mode-line** (bottom) | `read` | long-lived; emits line-oriented output (plain text, or the JSON **metadata channel** below) |
| **minimap** (right, optional) | `minimap` | long-lived; output streams into a right-hand pane (a scrolling buffer or an auto-detected JSON tree) |
| **events** (optional) | `events` | the **event channel** (UI -> client): the wrapper *writes* one JSON UI-event per line (key/submit/resize/focus/scroll) to this target's stdin |

For a `repl`-pragma / `cmk repl` session these default to the generic core dispatcher
(`tux.repl.kernel`); the pragma accepts an object with `read` / `eval` / `print` / `exit_after` /
`minimap` / `events` / `complete` keys to override them.

#### Beyond a make-REPL: general TUI clients
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

The wrapper is a metadata-driven **TUI host**, not just a make debugger.  A `read` target emits one JSON
object per line whose keys are routed to UI sections, so a client can drive a form, wizard, dashboard, or
custom-language REPL without touching the Go wrapper:

* **Chrome / layout**: `prompt` (custom input prompt), `input` / `modeline` / `panel` (tri-state bools;
  omit = visible), `split` (minimap width), `title` (window title).
* **Widgets**: `progress` `{pct,label,color,show}` (a determinate bar beside the spinner) and `toast`
  `{text,level,ttl}` (a transient top-right notification).
* **Minimaps**: `mm_config` `{pos:mode}` over positions `top|middle|bottom`, each mode `tree`
  (a lipgloss tree), `buffer` (a scrolling log), or `mode` (an overwrite snapshot), fed by
  `[<pos>_]mm_{tree|buffer|top|bottom}`.
* **Event channel**: wire an `events` target to *react* to the UI (the inverse of `read`).
* **Pluggable completion**: a `complete` target overrides the built-in `cmk cli complete` (any language).
* **Rich output**: target ANSI color is preserved in the panel, so a client renders markdown/syntax
  itself (e.g. piping through `glow`) and the wrapper passes it through.

See {{macros.repo_link('demos/cmk/repl-form.cmk', name='demos/cmk/repl-form.cmk', github=github)}} for a
non-debugger client exercising the chrome keys, the progress bar, and the event channel end-to-end.

A custom **minimap** feeder is another worked client:
{{macros.repo_link('demos/cmk/stack-of-stacks.cmk', name='demos/cmk/stack-of-stacks.cmk', github=github)}}
wires a `minimap` target that reads each branch's control-stack and emits tree nodes, so the right-hand
pane renders a live `fork` as sibling stacks under one root, a running / done marker per branch:

{{macros.img_link("stack-of-stacks.gif", mkdocs, "90%", class="cli_output")}}

#### VM debugger (a worked client)
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

A live **VM debugger** is a worked `read` client: the `tux.repl.debugger.feed` read-target translates the
VM's `__vm__.frames` into four regions: a mode-line (instruction pointer + clock), a last-command buffer,
a live call-stack **tree**, and an env buffer.  The frame machinery lives in the
[VM module]({{_}}/modules/virtual-machine).

{{macros.img_link("repl-debugger.gif", mkdocs, "90%", class="cli_output")}}

The demo wires that feed against a nested build pipeline as the debuggee:

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/debugger-example.cmk', type='cmk', github=github)}}

#### Notes
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

* **Import with `require`.**  The harness's children re-parse the importing file, so import the plugin
  by calling the `__plugins__.require` macro on `tux.repl.cmk` (idempotent and child-safe) rather than a bare
  include; the children inherit the registry and skip the re-import.
* **Plain Makefiles** can use the `tux.repl/<comma,separated,target,list>` helper target directly (no
  cmk-lang required); the `.cmk` paths use the `repl` pragma / `cmk repl`.
* **Build-on-first-use.**  The Go wrapper is materialized from inline block-refs and cross-built on
  first launch, via the [Golang Polyglot plugin]({{_}}/plugins/code.golang)'s
  build-if-needed pipeline; then cached per-host; later launches just exec the cached binary.

